   "text": "I read this as the oxacephem antibiotic flomoxef and wrote the SMILES region by region.\n\n- **Core:** a \u03b2-lactam fused to a six-membered ring containing one ring oxygen (the oxacephem skeleton). The ring carries a C=C bearing the carboxylic acid on one carbon and the CH2 linker on the other.\n- **C7 of the \u03b2-lactam:** it carries both a methoxy group and an acylamino group. The acyl group is NH\u2013C(=O)\u2013CH2\u2013S\u2013CHF2, i.e. a difluoromethylthio-acetamide.\n- **Side chain on the ring C=C:** a CH2\u2013S link to a tetrazole carbon. The tetrazole is a five-membered ring of one carbon and four nitrogens, and its N1 bears a CH2CH2OH group.\n- **Stereochemistry:** the drawing shows a wedge on the C7\u2013N bond and a hashed H on the ring-fusion carbon (C6). I worked out the @/@@ labels from the 2D coordinates. C7 is `[C@]` with neighbour order O, ring-closure C8 (the \u03b2-lactam C=O), N, C6. C6 is `[C@H]` with neighbour order C7, H, N (via the ring closure), O. This assignment was done by hand and has not been checked with software.\n\nSMILES: CO[C@]1(NC(=O)CSC(F)F)[C@H]2OCC(CSc3nnnn3CCO)=C(N2C1=O)C(=O)O"
